---
// /guidelines/: the community guidelines (night phase 16, E4; docs/POLICIES.md): how to take part in a
// paper's conversation well. A draft for the owner's review. Static; science.css only.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PolicyDraft from "../components/PolicyDraft.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../config";
---
<Base
  title="Community guidelines"
  description="How to take part in the conversation about a paper's code: about the code and the paper, with evidence, kindly."
  breadcrumb={[{ text: "Community guidelines" }]}
>
  <div class="policy">
    <h1>Community guidelines</h1>
    <PolicyDraft drafted="2026-09-29" />
    <p class="summary">
      {SITE_NAME} helps people read, check, reuse and improve research code. Many researchers do not publish
      their code, so be generous and precise with those who do.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Discuss the code and the paper. A comment such as "Line 42 filters at order 4; §2.3 says 2" helps. Do not judge the authors.</li>
      <li>Bring evidence. A mismatch names the paragraph and the lines; a reproduction failure says what you ran, on what data and environment, and what came out. The research issue forms ask for it.</li>
      <li>Assume good faith. Most mismatches are honest mistakes or undocumented choices. Ask before concluding.</li>
      <li>Give credit. Cite papers by their DOI and code by its commit; say where an idea or a fix comes from.</li>
      <li>Keep private things private. Do not post someone's contact details, data about patients or participants, or a secret key.</li>
      <li>Stay on topic. Open one issue per problem; a paper's discussion is about that paper.</li>
      <li>Accept a "no". Authors and maintainers decide what goes into their code; they may lock a heated conversation or limit interactions for a while.</li>
      <li>If someone behaves badly, use the "Report" link or block them, and do not retaliate. The owner reads every report.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      What is not allowed at all is in the <a href="/acceptable-use/">acceptable-use policy</a>; what happens then
      is in the <a href="/terms/">terms</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
